      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "title": "What is a fragment, exactly, and what do we call the thing that loads into a node?",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "library-made-current",
          "nodeId": "library-made-current-q-fragment"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/classes/nodes/task-planning/open-question.node-class.node",
        "owner": "Michael",
        "status": "answered",
        "body": "Michael, 2026-10-28: a fragment is a piece of a larger graph that expands through a portal. The portal is only the mechanism; a graph that opens as a fragment loads more data into the same node, so it should not behave exactly as a portal does. The terminology is not worked out. In the code \"fragment\" currently means three things: a graph with no root (being renamed), a piece extracted from a graph (utils/fragmentExtraction.js, intent.kind \"fragment\"), and the portal behaviour \"Expand Fragment\" with fragment focus. To settle: the name for the expand-in-place behaviour, how it differs from a portal in the model and in the editor, and whether extraction is the same thing seen from the other side.",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1793219056199,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        },
        "answeredAt": "2026-10-28T20:57:57.755Z",
        "resolution": "Michael, 2026-10-28",
        "answer": "A fragment is a piece of another file loaded into the graph you are in: its nodes join this graph's node set, sit inside this graph's membrane, can be joined by edges to the nodes around them, and are edited back to their one source file. A portal is a door into another universe (another graph, with its own membrane). The portal is only the mechanism for reaching the file. Fragment is its own thing, not a Portal behaviour. A shared nodeId is identity (the expanded node), never a join between open graphs. Fragments follow the class and instance split: the file is the class, loading makes an instance in the host (see the task \"Fragments are not portals\")."
      }
    },
