      "data": {
        "title": "Fragments are not portals",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "library-made-current",
          "nodeId": "library-made-current-fragments-apart"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/classes/nodes/task-planning/task.node-class.node",
        "owner": "Michael",
        "status": "in-progress",
        "body": "Decided 2026-10-28 (see the answered question): a portal opens another graph; a fragment adds to the graph you are in. Today they are the same thing: \"Expand Fragment\" is a Portal behaviour (GraphEditorContent.js, the portal editor), an expansion is also a Bridge node with mode \"expand\" (validateGraphInvariants EXPANSION_BRIDGE_NODE_ID_REQUIRED), and the Portal primitive describes the pair. To do: (1) a name and a primitive for the inclusion (fragment); (2) take Expand Fragment out of the Portal's behaviours and the editor's portal options; (3) say in the Portal and Bridge primitives what is left; (4) decide the membrane rule: fragment nodes belong to the host, so edges cross freely; (5) the fragment authoring context, save (excludeExpanded) and fragmentFocus already work as inclusion and should keep working; (6) write the fragment primitive as a canon page (source of truth) and update the Root primitive's two-roots wording. Done already: the workspace no longer merges two open graphs into one frame because they share a root nodeId. Nothing else is changed until Michael has seen the shape. DIRECTION (Michael, 2026-10-28, \"go with your rule\"): fragments follow the class and instance split. The fragment file is the class (a section authored once, no doors of its own, a root that only describes it, intent.kind \"fragment\"); loading it into a graph makes an instance, whose nodes become that graph's nodes under that graph's imports, with host-local ids and the class id kept as provenance (today the source ids are kept, so loading one fragment twice into a graph would collide). Primarily for loading a big graph in pieces (the host owns the section), also for reuse. Who owns the section decides what an edit does: loaded into the graph that owns it, edits are written back to the file; loaded into any other graph, edits are overrides on the instance, the file is unchanged, and a refresh re-applies the overrides over the new class. Same shape as an export Bridge handing on a class. Check before building: whether Collection windows (windowed-collection-member) already cover loading in pieces.\n\nPROGRESS 2026-10-29. Done: the Fragment primitive is a canon page (library/primitives/fragment) with a working example pair beside it; a fragment is a piece of a node, and a node may have more than two; the stand-in has an Expand/Collapse button and is no longer a way through (click, double-click and the workspace handle do not open its graph); a piece brings content and structure only, and code and Bridges are withheld unless the host's own stand-in grants them in target.allow, by type or by one Bridge's address; the Portal, Bridge and Root pages say where a Fragment stands. Still to do: (2) take Expand out of the Portal and onto the Import end of a Bridge (\"Include as fragment\"); the class and instance direction above; who owns an edit to an included node; whether a piece's root should list the Bridges it wants so the host can see them; more than two pieces (only a pair has been tried).\n\nUPDATE 2026-10-30. Decided and built: Fragment is its own node type (fragment), not a Portal and not an Import. It is drawn by the Portal's card and draws a face of the other piece like a Portal; an Expand/Collapse button sits on its title bar; the Portal has no expand mode and its editor no longer offers Expand Fragment; fragment extraction makes Fragment nodes. So item (2) above is done, and the \"Include as fragment on the Import end of a Bridge\" idea is dropped. Still open: the class and instance direction; who owns an edit to an included node; whether a piece's root should list the Bridges it wants; more than two pieces. Order to go live: deploy the app, then publish canon (the example pair uses the new type).",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1793221077755,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        }
      }
