      "data": {
        "title": "Events across a Bridge",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "library-made-current",
          "nodeId": "library-made-current-events-across-a-bridge"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/classes/nodes/task-planning/task.node-class.node",
        "owner": "Michael",
        "status": "in-progress",
        "body": "Michael, 2026-10-28: graphs that are structurally adjacent, meaning they share a Bridge, should pay attention to each other, and an event should reach several listeners. The Event Handler retirement of 2026-10-16 (862f3428) removed the cross-graph part with it (bridgeEventSubscriptions.js, the Bridge events panel, five test suites); Michael meant only to make Methods easy to fire. Nothing in canon or the library depends on it. Design (agreed in shape): the emitter declares its event as now (port event:<key>); an export lists the events it offers (exposure.events, an allowlist, like exposure.methods); an import of that graph shows them as event ports; the importing graph draws ordinary invokes edges from those ports to its own Methods; when the emitter fires and both graphs are open, every importing graph with such an edge runs its Methods, in its own graph with its own declared effects. Only graphs sharing a Bridge are reached; each listener opts in by its edge. To do: (1) the model in the Import and Export primitive page (the Known gaps paragraph already says designed, not built); (2) dispatcher: find open instances whose imports name the emitter graph, and run their invokes Methods (utils/eventDelivery.js); (3) imports draw event ports (utils/dynamicPortDeclarations.js); (4) an events section on the export in the editor; (5) validator: an invokes edge from an import event port needs the export to offer it; (6) tests; (7) the canon edge class \"listens\" still describes Event Handlers (retired): retire or rewrite it with this; (8) the bridge-template/import graph in the personal library carries old subscriptions.\n\nPROGRESS 2026-10-30. Built: (2) the dispatcher delivers to open graphs that import the emitter (utils/bridgeEvents.js, eventDelivery.js); (3) an import draws a port for each event in its own events list (deriveBridgeEventPorts); (4) the Bridge Editor has an Events section on both ends (BridgeEventsEditorSection.js); (6) tests/bridgeEvents.test.js; and a Method run for a graph that is not in focus writes into that graph with updateNode (usePluginRuntime.js), any other change refused. Smoke pair: graphs/bridge-events-smoke (an emitter and a listener whose faces show the live value), checked live in the workspace. AGENTS.md says so. Still to do: (1) done: the canon wording on the Import and Export page (written 2026-10-30; goes live when canon is published); (5) done in part (2026-10-30, 9ad7954a): the validator warns on a Bridge end's malformed event key (BRIDGE_EVENT_KEY_MALFORMED) and on an export that offers an event its own graph does not declare (EXPORT_EVENT_NOT_DECLARED), both warnings only. It cannot check that an imported graph really offers an event, because the validator works on one file and would have to fetch the other; the Bridge Editor does that instead and marks a chosen event the source no longer declares (\"not declared any more\"). An invokes edge from an import event port already needs the port, and the port exists only while the import lists the event; (7) the canon edge class \"listens\" still describes Event Handlers; (8) the old subscriptions in the personal library bridge graph; a listener Method may use only updateNode in a graph that is not in focus; face buttons that change a value from a graph tile do not exist (controls act on the node a view belongs to).",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
